 ### DMTet Surface Reconstruction with Eikonal Regularization
-This variant defines object geometry as a Signed Distance Field (SDF) on a tetrahedral mesh $T$. Each vertex $v_i$ has an SDF value $S_i \in \mathbb{R}$ and a deformation value $\Delta v_i \in \mathbb{R}^3$. The mesh is updated as $v_i' = v_i + \Delta v_i$ [S3]. It supervises the optimization using rendered masks derived from projecting the object according to camera poses [S3]. It uses progressive position encoding to alleviate high-frequency noise while retaining details, gradually increasing the encoding frequency after training reaches a certain stage [S3]. It adds Implicit Geometric Regularization (IGR) as an eikonal loss to constrain the gradient magnitude of the SDF field $f_\theta$ to be close to 1: $L_{eikonal} = E_{v_0 \in V_0} (||\nabla f_\theta (v_0 )||^2 - 1)^2$ [S3].
+This variant defines object geometry as a Signed Distance Field (SDF) on a tetrahedral mesh $T$ [S3]. Each vertex $v_i$ has an SDF value $S_i \in \mathbb{R}$ and a deformation value $\Delta v_i \in \mathbb{R}^3$ [S3]. The mesh is updated as $v_i' = v_i + \Delta v_i$ [S3]. It supervises the optimization using rendered masks derived from projecting the object according to camera poses [S3]. It uses progressive position encoding to alleviate high-frequency noise while retaining details, gradually increasing the encoding frequency after training reaches a certain stage [S3]. It adds Implicit Geometric Regularization (IGR) as an eikonal loss to constrain the gradient magnitude of the SDF field $f_\theta$ to be close to 1: $L_{eikonal} = E_{v_0 \in V_0} (||\nabla f_\theta (v_0 )||^2 - 1)^2$ [S3].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of optimization iterations and the cost of rendering/gradient computation per iteration. Gradient descent requires multiple forward and backward passes per step [G].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of optimization iterations and the cost of rendering/gradient computation per iteration [G]. Gradient descent requires multiple forward and backward passes per step [G].
 *   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the resolution of the SDF grid or the number of vertices in the tetrahedral mesh, plus the memory for storing gradients and intermediate rendering buffers [G].
 *   **Strengths:** Can recover detailed geometry from sparse views; differentiable nature allows end-to-end optimization with other neural components; eikonal regularization helps maintain smooth, well-defined surfaces [G].
